    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Meno is named after Mennonite leader Menno Simons, though the founder accidentally omitted one letter from the name on the post office application.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meno%2C_Oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Many of the original inhabitants of Meno were Mennonites of Polish and Russian descent.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meno%2C_Oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Enid and Anadarko Railway constructed a line through Meno between 1901 and 1902 to connect Enid and Greenfield.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meno%2C_Oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Meno was the original home of the Oklahoma Bible Academy from 1911 until the school moved to Enid in 1983.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meno%2C_Oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "By 1918, the town of Meno boasted a bank, a flour mill, two creameries, and two grain elevators.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meno%2C_Oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Tributaries of Hoyle Creek drain the town of Meno to the south, eventually flowing into the Cimarron River.",
